WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.230
- [Host] There's a reason the carpet

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:03.960
at this New York City Premiere is blue.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.850
The studio behind this new series

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.940
isn't Universal or Paramount
Pictures, it's Walmart.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.363
- Don't look at me like, like that.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:17.580
♪ It's all right 'cause
you're all I need ♪

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.160
- [Host] Walmart says, this
isn't a typical romcom,

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.420
it's romcommerce.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.550
- Just decided this is so me.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.560
- And it's called "Add to Heart."

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.090
Viewers can buy those items

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.295
and over 330 others

00:00:28.295 --> 00:00:31.200
directly from Walmart while
watching the series on

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.060
TikTok, Roku and YouTube.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.620
- This is a big bet for us.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.510
- [Host] Here's why
Walmart and other companies

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.030
are investing in so-called
shoppable content

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.823
even though the trend has
yet to take off in the US.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.690
The series follows the
typical holiday romcom plot.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.670
City girl returns to her hometown

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.203
and bumps into an old flame.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:50.903
- Jess.

00:00:50.903 --> 00:00:51.736
- Javi?

00:00:51.736 --> 00:00:53.400
- There's a little bit of
cheesiness within a romantic

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.130
comedy of course, but there's
nothing cheesy in the selling

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.420
and it feels very seamless

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.640
and natural in how it comes together.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.040
- [Host] On TikTok, each
three minute episode runs

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.880
as a paid in-feed ad featuring
10 shoppable product tiles.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.330
Roku users see an ad on their
home screen, which takes them

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.790
to the series playlist while watching,

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.500
they can shop for products by pressing,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.970
okay on their Roku remote.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.250
- Babe, can I come out now?

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.470
- This allows users to
text themselves a link

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:23.010
to shop on their phone, on
YouTube, users can find a link

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.170
to all the products in
the description box.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.900
- You like this unpacking
for my Miami trip?

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.270
- [Host] Almost everything
in the 23-part series

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.110
from the outfits

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.000
to the decor is from Walmart.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.240
- Products were chosen from
things that are giftable, things

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.700
that we think are really
on trend for the season.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.110
- If they wanted to see the jacket

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.280
and then also the outfit under it.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.630
It might be like we come into the scene

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.490
with the jacket and then we take it off.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:45.840
It's 10 degrees outside.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:46.673
- What did you say?

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:48.210
- There's a shot, it's in the trailer

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.193
with like walking up to the door.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.130
That was an example of
a scene where we had

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.560
to really get it right, like
the placement of the shoes had

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.300
to be very exact.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.450
- Merry Christmas babe.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.492
- [Host] To measure consumer engagement,

00:02:02.492 --> 00:02:03.325
Walmart looks at three things.

00:02:03.325 --> 00:02:06.311
One, are people adding
the item to their cart?

00:02:06.311 --> 00:02:07.223
- Who proposes after six months?

00:02:07.223 --> 00:02:09.450
Does he even know your middle name?

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.190
- [Host] Two, are they buying it

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.110
and three, if they're not buying it,

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.580
do they come back to it later?

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.660
- If the scoop boots was
something that you added

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.000
to your cart but then
abandoned, but then went

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.070
and bought them in
store later, we consider

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.305
that inspiration moment happening from the

00:02:25.305 --> 00:02:27.277
content that they first saw there.

00:02:27.277 --> 00:02:29.370
- [Host] "Add to Heart"
is the newest iteration of

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.100
what Walmart calls its
social commerce strategy.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.740
The company has also
partnered with NBC/Universal

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.260
to create a shoppable
experience in season eight

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.020
of Bravo's Reality Show
"Below Deck Mediterranean"

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:40.853
on Peacock.

00:02:40.853 --> 00:02:42.145
- What do you mean I'm not on top?

00:02:42.145 --> 00:02:44.083
- Well, you're obviously not.

00:02:44.083 --> 00:02:45.510
- [Host] During ad breaks,
viewers get served an ad

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.950
for a Walmart product similar

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.870
to the one they just saw in the show,

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.690
and they can buy it using
the QR code on screen.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.970
- Let's get Merry and Bright.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.620
- [Host] Other companies
like Home Depot have

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.000
also dabbled in this space.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.170
- Let's get to decorating.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.157
- [Host] It created a
shoppable series called

00:03:00.157 --> 00:03:01.710
"Merry and Bright" where viewers

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:05.731
can buy items using integrated
QR codes, and a number

00:03:05.731 --> 00:03:08.940
of companies have been using
TikTok's video shopping ads,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.470
which launched last year.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.894
- You have every player in
the ecosystem thinking we have

00:03:12.894 --> 00:03:16.290
to be able to unlock
more shopping activity.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:17.190
- [Host] Christopher Vollmer

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.110
is a managing director at MediaLink,

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.060
a media and marketing consulting firm.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.920
Walmart is a MediaLink client.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.980
Vollmer, however, does not
personally advise Walmart.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.990
- You've got media
companies that are trying

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:30.728
to unlock new sources of
revenue and you've got brands

00:03:30.728 --> 00:03:33.210
and retailers that are trying to be

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:34.290
where all the attention is,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.900
which is social media
platforms and streaming.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.730
- [Host] Vollmer says
Apple's privacy change

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:39.889
in 2021 that restricted

00:03:39.889 --> 00:03:42.147
how users are tracked, also
triggered many companies

00:03:42.147 --> 00:03:45.360
to move away from cookie-based ads.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.910
- What they're trying to do
now is build up much more

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.420
of a first party data
relationship with consumers

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.070
and actually push them more

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.750
and more to their own environments

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.580
where they can enact a transaction

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.100
and build more of a
long-term relationship.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.930
- [Host] Shoppable content
is popular in China,

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.210
but it's still in its
early days in the US.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.220
- It has been a slower ramp in the US

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:06.480
but it is definitely ramping.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.830
It's taken off a little bit more

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.990
with younger generations first,

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:13.020
and I think we're just developing
the norms in the routines

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.740
and the behavior here in the US right now.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.150
- The content has to be great.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.772
It can be a fantastic user experience,

00:04:20.772 --> 00:04:22.770
but if it's not embedded in a story

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.590
that users find a very
compelling, very interesting,

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.187
it's not gonna be something
that they come back to.

00:04:28.187 --> 00:04:30.270
- Javi, I have something to tell you.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.380
- [Host] Walmart didn't share any

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.360
data about its social commerce efforts.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.940
- Do not get me started
about the color of this bag.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.890
- [Host] But White says
there's been more traffic

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.819
to the Walmart site and increased sales.

00:04:39.819 --> 00:04:42.849
- I think we will pick up a
lot of learnings along the way

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:44.423
that will continue to inform

00:04:44.423 --> 00:04:47.553
and help us progress down
this shoppable journey.

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:48.593
- Mr. Bogle, is it possible

00:04:48.593 --> 00:04:51.000
to add a stop at Walmart on the way?

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.119
- Absolutely.

00:04:52.119 --> 00:04:54.786
(upbeat music)

